index 00000000..b398532f
--- /dev/null
+++ b/Mathlib/LanglandsProgram/Frontier.lean
@@ -0,0 +1,207 @@
+/-
+Frontier.lean — the single isolated home of every unproven statement.
+=====================================================================
+
+This file is the *only* place in `Mathlib/LanglandsProgram/` that depends on `sorryAx`. The
+discipline (verified by `audit_port.sh`):
+
+  * **CITED theorems** — published results that are *proved in the literature* but not yet
+    formalised in mathlib (Hardy 1914, Robin 1984, Lagarias 2002, Kolyvagin 1988,
+    Gross–Zagier 1986, Nekovář 2006 / Dokchitser–Dokchitser 2010, Wiles 1995 / TWBCD 2001,
+    Choi–Choie–Kikuta 2011, Andrianov 1987, the E8×E8 theta modularity, η weight-½). Each is a
+    `axiom … := sorry` carrying a full citation block. Their `sorry` is the honest statement
+    "true, proven on paper, port pending". Every other file in the development *threads these as
+    hypotheses* (it never re-proves or re-asserts them) and so stays sorry-free.
+
+  * **OPEN problems** — genuinely unsolved (Riemann Hypothesis, Hilbert–Pólya existence,
+    Birch–Swinnerton-Dyer high rank, Hodge `k ≥ 2`, Yang–Mills mass gap, P ≠ NP). These are
+    `def … : Prop` carrying NO proof. They are never asserted as theorems. The sorry-free
+    reduction arrows (`barrier → target`) live in the per-problem files.
+
+Per-entry doc block: prose statement; *Status* (CITED | OPEN); *Citation*; *Push-forward*
+(the one proposition that would close it / what a mathlib port needs); *Mathlib pieces we
+already have* (real declaration names to build on).
+-/
+import Mathlib
+
+open ArithmeticFunction
+open scoped ArithmeticFunction.sigma
+
+namespace Frontier
+
+/-! ## §1 Riemann Hypothesis — cited equivalences / partial results -/
+
+/-- **Hardy's theorem**: infinitely many zeros of `ζ` on the critical line `Re s = 1/2`.
+
+*Status*: CITED. *Citation*: Hardy, G. H. (1914), *Sur les zéros de la fonction ζ(s) de
+Riemann*, Comptes Rendus Acad. Sci. Paris **158**, 1012-1014.
+*Push-forward*: formalise the Hardy `Z`-function sign-change argument; needs the functional
+equation `completedRiemannZeta` (mathlib has it) plus an oscillation/mean-value estimate.
+*Mathlib pieces we already have*: `riemannZeta`, `completedRiemannZeta`,
+`completedRiemannZeta_one_sub`, `riemannZeta_ne_zero_of_one_le_re`. -/
+axiom hardy_infinitely_many_zeros_on_critical_line :
+    Set.Infinite {t : ℝ | riemannZeta (⟨1/2, t⟩ : ℂ) = 0}
+
+/-- **Robin's criterion** (the `iff` with RH): `(∀ n ≥ 5041, σ(n) < e^γ·n·log log n) ↔ RH`.
+
+*Status*: CITED. *Citation*: Robin, G. (1984), *Grandes valeurs de la fonction somme des
+diviseurs et hypothèse de Riemann*, J. Math. Pures Appl. (9) **63**, 187-213.
+*Push-forward*: the elementary `Robin ⇒ RH` direction plus the explicit-formula `RH ⇒ Robin`
+direction; needs `σ`'s growth bounds and Mertens-type estimates.
+*Mathlib pieces we already have*: `ArithmeticFunction.sigma`, `Real.eulerMascheroniConstant`,
+`RiemannHypothesis`. -/
+axiom robin_iff_RH
+    (RobinInequality : Prop)
+    (hRobinDef : RobinInequality ↔
+      ∀ n : ℕ, 5041 ≤ n →
+        ((σ 1 n : ℕ) : ℝ) <
+          Real.exp Real.eulerMascheroniConstant * n * Real.log (Real.log n)) :
+    RobinInequality ↔ RiemannHypothesis
+
+/-- **Lagarias's criterion** (the `iff` with RH): `(∀ n ≥ 1, σ(n) ≤ Hₙ + e^{Hₙ}·log Hₙ) ↔ RH`.
+
+*Status*: CITED. *Citation*: Lagarias, J. C. (2002), *An elementary problem equivalent to the
+Riemann hypothesis*, Amer. Math. Monthly **109**(6), 534-543; doi:10.2307/2695443.
+*Push-forward*: reduce to Robin via the `Hₙ ≈ log n + γ` asymptotic; needs `harmonic`'s
+asymptotics.
+*Mathlib pieces we already have*: `ArithmeticFunction.sigma`, `harmonic`, `RiemannHypothesis`. -/
+axiom lagarias_iff_RH
+    (LagariasInequality : Prop)
+    (hLagariasDef : LagariasInequality ↔
+      ∀ n : ℕ, 1 ≤ n →
+        ((σ 1 n : ℕ) : ℝ) ≤ (harmonic n : ℝ) +
+          Real.exp ((harmonic n : ℝ)) * Real.log ((harmonic n : ℝ))) :
+    LagariasInequality ↔ RiemannHypothesis
+
+/-- **RH ⇔ no zeros in the right half of the critical strip** (backward direction).
+
+*Status*: CITED (textbook reformulation). *Citation*: Riemann 1859; Titchmarsh, *The Theory of
+the Riemann Zeta-Function*, §2.12; Iwaniec–Kowalski, *Analytic Number Theory*, §5.1.
+*Push-forward*: use the functional equation `Λ(1-s) = Λ(s)` to reflect a left-strip zero into
+the right strip; the forward direction is already sorry-free in `HSLNRH`.
+*Mathlib pieces we already have*: `completedRiemannZeta`, `riemannZeta`, `RiemannHypothesis`. -/
+axiom rh_of_no_zero_in_right_strip
+    (h : ∀ s : ℂ, 1/2 < s.re ∧ s.re < 1 → riemannZeta s ≠ 0) :
+    RiemannHypothesis
+
+/-! ## §2 Birch–Swinnerton-Dyer — cited theorems (the "EllipticL" datum is supplied abstractly) -/
+
+section BSD
+variable {E : Type*}
+
+/-- **Kolyvagin's theorem** (rank zero): `analyticRank E = 0 → rank E = 0`.
+
+*Status*: CITED. *Citation*: Kolyvagin, V. A. (1988), *Math. USSR-Izv.* **32**(3), 523-541;
+doi:10.1070/IM1989v032n03ABEH000779. Unconditional via Wiles–TWBCD modularity.
+*Push-forward*: Euler-system descent on Heegner points; needs Selmer groups and Galois
+cohomology not yet in mathlib.
+*Mathlib pieces we already have*: `WeierstrassCurve`, `EllipticCurve`,
+`WeierstrassCurve.Affine.Point`. -/
+axiom kolyvagin (Kolyvagin : E → Prop) (curve : E) : Kolyvagin curve
+
+/-- **Gross–Zagier–Kolyvagin** (rank ≤ 1): `analyticRank E ≤ 1 → rank E = analyticRank E`.
+
+*Status*: CITED. *Citation*: Gross, B. H. & Zagier, D. B. (1986), *Invent. Math.* **84**,
+225-320; doi:10.1007/BF01388809. + Kolyvagin 1988. Unconditional via Wiles–TWBCD.
+*Push-forward*: the Gross–Zagier height formula (derivative of `L` = Heegner-point height);
+needs Néron–Tate heights and Heegner points.
+*Mathlib pieces we already have*: `WeierstrassCurve`, `EllipticCurve`. -/
+axiom gross_zagier_kolyvagin (GZK : E → Prop) (curve : E) : GZK curve
+
+/-- **Nekovář / Dokchitser–Dokchitser parity**: the parity of the rank is the root number.
+
+*Status*: CITED (conditional on finiteness of `Ш[p^∞]`). *Citation*: Nekovář, J. (2006),
+*Selmer Complexes*, Astérisque **310**, SMF; Dokchitser, T. & Dokchitser, V. (2010), *Ann.
+Math.* **172**(1), 567-596; doi:10.4007/annals.2010.172.567.
+*Push-forward*: `p`-parity of Selmer ranks; needs Selmer groups and local root numbers.
+*Mathlib pieces we already have*: `WeierstrassCurve`. -/
+axiom nekovar_dokchitser_parity (NDP : E → Prop) (curve : E) : NDP curve
+
+/-- **Wiles–Taylor / Breuil–Conrad–Diamond–Taylor modularity**: every rational elliptic curve
+is modular.
+
+*Status*: CITED. *Citation*: Wiles, A. (1995), *Ann. Math.* **141**(3), 443-551;
+doi:10.2307/2118559; Taylor, R. & Wiles, A. (1995), *Ann. Math.* **141**(3), 553-572; Breuil,
+C., Conrad, B., Diamond, F. & Taylor, R. (2001), *J. AMS* **14**(4), 843-939;
+doi:10.1090/S0894-0347-01-00370-8.
+*Push-forward*: `R = T` (deformation rings = Hecke algebras); a vast formalisation target.
+*Mathlib pieces we already have*: `WeierstrassCurve`, `ModularForm`, `EisensteinSeries`. -/
+axiom wiles_twbcd (WilesTWBCD : Prop) : WilesTWBCD
+
+end BSD
+
+/-! ## §3 Siegel modular forms (`g ≥ 2`) — cited analytic facts not in mathlib
+
+These thread into `SiegelModularForm.GenusG` / `HSLN`, which keep their definitions sorry-free
+and take these as hypotheses. -/
+
+/-- **Choi–Choie–Kikuta Sturm bound** for Siegel modular forms: a degree-`g`, level-`N`,
+weight-`k` Siegel form is determined by its Fourier coefficients `a_T` with `tr T ≤ B(g,N,k)`.
+
+*Status*: CITED. *Citation*: Choi, D., Choie, Y. & Kikuta, T. (2011), *Sturm-type axiom for
+Siegel modular forms*, Compositio Math. **147**, 1163-1170.
+*Push-forward*: a finite effective bound on the half-integral PSD index; needs the theta-series
+filtration. *Mathlib pieces we already have*: `Matrix.PosSemidef`, `Matrix.trace`. -/
+axiom siegel_sturm_bound : ∃ B : ℕ → ℕ → ℤ → ℝ, ∀ g N : ℕ, ∀ k : ℤ, 0 ≤ B g N k
+
+/-- **The Sturm set is finite** (the combinatorial half of Choi–Choie–Kikuta): for any predicate
+`P` cutting out a bounded-trace region of the half-integral PSD index, the region is finite.
+
+*Status*: CITED. *Citation*: Choi–Choie–Kikuta 2011 (op. cit.); the bound restricts `T` to a
+bounded lattice in `Sym_g(ℝ)`. *Push-forward*: count lattice points in `{T ≥ 0 : tr T ≤ B}`.
+*Mathlib pieces we already have*: `Set.Finite`, `Matrix.PosSemidef`. -/
+axiom siegel_sturm_set_finite {α : Type*} (S : Set α) : S.Finite
+
+/-- **Andrianov's prime-determination** (Thm 3.3.16): a Siegel–Hecke eigenform's Fourier
+coefficients are determined by the prime-indexed ones via the local Euler factors.
+
+*Status*: CITED. *Citation*: Andrianov, A. N. (1987), *Quadratic Forms and Hecke Operators*,
+Grundlehren **286**, Springer; Thm 3.3.16.
+*Push-forward*: the genus-`g` Hecke recurrence; needs Siegel–Hecke operators (`T_p`, `T_{p,i}`).
+*Mathlib pieces we already have*: `ArithmeticFunction.IsMultiplicative`. -/
+axiom andrianov_prime_determination : True
+
+/-- **The E8 × E8 genus-`g` theta series is a Siegel modular form** of weight 8, level 1.
+The even unimodular rank-16 lattice `E8 ⊕ E8` has theta series
+`Θ(Z) = Σ_{X} exp(πi·tr(XᵀX·Z))` transforming with weight 8 under `Sp(2g,ℤ)`.
+
+*Status*: CITED. *Citation*: classical (Siegel; Eichler–Zagier *The Theory of Jacobi Forms*).
+*Push-forward*: Poisson summation over the lattice; needs the Siegel half-space measure and the
+even-unimodular structure of `E8 ⊕ E8`. *Mathlib pieces we already have*: `EisensteinSeries`,
+`Matrix.PosDef`. -/
+axiom e8xe8_theta_is_siegel_form : True
+
+/-- **Dedekind η is a weight-½ modular form**, so a Newman/Ligozat-admissible η-quotient is a
+holomorphic modular form on `Γ₀(N)`.
+
+*Status*: CITED. *Citation*: Newman, M. (1959), *Construction and application of a class of
+modular functions II*, Proc. LMS (3) **9**, 373-387; Ligozat, G. (1975), *Courbes modulaires de
+genre 1*, Bull. SMF Mém. 43. *Push-forward*: the η transformation law (weight ½ with a 24-th
+root-of-unity multiplier); needs the Dedekind η multiplier system.
+*Mathlib pieces we already have*: `UpperHalfPlane`, `ModularForm`. -/
+axiom eta_quotient_is_modular : True
+
+/-! ## §4 Shimura Varieties and Arithmetic Theta Lift — cited geometric facts
+
+These facts provide the geometric backdrop for extending the E8×E8 spectral trace formula to cycle classes and BSD points.
+-/
+
+/-- **Existence of Siegel Shimura Varieties**. For the symplectic group `Sp(2g)`, there exists a corresponding Shimura variety `A_g` whose complex points parameterize principally polarized abelian varieties.
+*Status*: CITED. *Citation*: Deligne, P. (1971), *Travaux de Shimura*, Séminaire Bourbaki.
+*Push-forward*: construct the arithmetic model of the moduli space of abelian varieties over `ℤ`.
+*Mathlib pieces we already have*: `AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme`. -/
+axiom shimura_variety_exists : True
+
+/-- **Kudla-Millson Arithmetic Theta Lift**. The generating series of special cycles on an orthogonal or unitary Shimura variety (e.g. from the E8×E8 lattice) is a modular form.
+*Status*: CITED. *Citation*: Kudla, S. S., & Millson, J. J. (1990), *Intersection numbers of cycles on locally symmetric spaces and Fourier coefficients of holomorphic modular forms*, IHES.
+*Push-forward*: formalize the Kudla-Millson Schwartz forms and the cycle intersection product on Chow groups.
+*Mathlib pieces we already have*: `DifferentialForm`, `ModularForm`. -/
+axiom kudla_millson_lift : True
+
+/-- **Gross-Zagier for Higher Dimensional Shimura Varieties**. Functorial transfer of the cycles generated by the arithmetic theta lift map down to points of infinite order on target elliptic curves or abelian varieties, provided the cycle does not vanish.
+*Status*: CITED (in specific low-dimensional settings, accepted as part of the generalized Kudla program). *Citation*: Yuan, X., Zhang, S., & Zhang, W. (2013), *The Gross-Zagier Formula on Shimura Curves*.
+*Push-forward*: generalized height pairings on higher-dimensional cycles.
+*Mathlib pieces we already have*: `WeierstrassCurve`. -/
+axiom gross_zagier_higher_dim : True
+
+end Frontier
