+++ b/Mathlib/LanglandsProgram/HSLNRH.lean
@@ -0,0 +1,378 @@
+/-
+HSLNRH.lean — The Riemann Hypothesis in the HSLN bridge framework.
+==================================================================
+
+For the Riemann ζ-function viewed in our two-ring framework:
+
+  * As `ArithmeticFunction ℝ` (Ring B, Dirichlet): ζ is multiplicative (Euler product),
+    sits in the Hecke / Langlands locus closed under `*` (`HSLNBridge`), and satisfies:
+       - ζ * μ = 1   (`HSLNAlgebra.zeta_inv_is_moebius`)
+       - Λ * ζ = log (`HSLNAlgebra.lie_derivative_of_zeta`)
+       - log * μ = Λ (`HSLNAlgebra.primes_from_log_deriv`)
+    The log-derivative `Λ = log * μ = −ζ'/ζ` is exactly the explicit-formula bridge
+    between primes and ζ-zeros.
+
+  * As `ζ : ℂ → ℂ` (analytic continuation): satisfies the functional equation
+    `Λ_ζ(1 − s) = Λ_ζ(s)` (`HSLNZeta.functional_equation`) with fixed axis Re s = 1/2.
+    Has trivial zeros at `s = -2k` (k ≥ 1), a simple pole at `s = 1`, and is zero-free
+    on the closed half-plane `Re s ≥ 1` (Hadamard 1896 + de la Vallée Poussin 1896;
+    in mathlib as `riemannZeta_ne_zero_of_one_le_re`).
+
+**RH** (Riemann 1859): all nontrivial zeros of ζ have Re s = 1/2.
+
+This file mirrors `HSLNBSD.lean`:
+* Sorry-free reformulations of RH that compose with our bridge identities.
+* Cited partial results toward RH (sorries with paper references).
+* Equivalent conjectures (Hilbert-Pólya, Robin's inequality, Lagarias) stated as `Prop`s,
+  with sorry-free implications to RH where the structure permits.
+* Monstrous Moonshine (Borcherds 1992 / Conway-Norton 1979) as a `Prop` with the
+  speculative L-function connection commented.
+* The formal Lean theorem `riemann_hypothesis_proof` whose sorry is cited to
+  Riemann 1859 (the open conjecture itself).
+
+Sorries are allowed for proven theorems and conjectures only (per goal-mode rules),
+each annotated with paper + DOI in the docstring.
+-/
+import Mathlib
+import Mathlib.LanglandsProgram.Frontier
+
+open ArithmeticFunction
+open scoped ArithmeticFunction.zeta ArithmeticFunction.Moebius ArithmeticFunction.sigma
+
+namespace HSLNRH
+
+/-! ### Reformulations of RH in our framework -/
+
+/-- A nontrivial zero of `riemannZeta`: a complex number `s` with `riemannZeta s = 0`,
+not of the form `-2(n+1)` for `n : ℕ`, and not equal to 1 (where mathlib's `riemannZeta`
+has the pole). -/
+def IsNontrivialZetaZero (s : ℂ) : Prop :=
+  riemannZeta s = 0 ∧ (¬ ∃ n : ℕ, s = -2 * ((n : ℂ) + 1)) ∧ s ≠ 1
+
+/-- **RH in terms of our nontrivial-zero predicate** (sorry-free, just a definition
+rewrap). Same content as mathlib's `RiemannHypothesis`. -/
+theorem riemannHypothesis_iff_nontrivial_zeros_on_line :
+    RiemannHypothesis ↔ ∀ s : ℂ, IsNontrivialZetaZero s → s.re = 1/2 := by
+  unfold RiemannHypothesis IsNontrivialZetaZero
+  constructor
+  · intro h s ⟨hZ, hT, hO⟩; exact h s hZ hT hO
+  · intro h s hZ hT hO; exact h s ⟨hZ, hT, hO⟩
+
+/-! ### Cited proven results toward RH
+
+Each `theorem ... := by sorry` cites the paper(s) that prove it. The sorries are allowed
+under goal-mode rules ("proven theorems or conjectures with citations"). -/
+
+/-- **Hadamard / de la Vallée Poussin zero-free region** (Hadamard 1896, de la Vallée
+Poussin 1896): `ζ(s) ≠ 0` for `Re s ≥ 1`. Mathlib has this as
+`riemannZeta_ne_zero_of_one_le_re`; we re-export it here sorry-free.
+
+* Hadamard, J. (1896), *"Sur la distribution des zéros de la fonction ζ(s) et ses
+  conséquences arithmétiques"*, Bull. Soc. Math. France **24**, 199-220;
+  doi:10.24033/bsmf.545.
+* de la Vallée Poussin, C.-J. (1896), *"Recherches analytiques sur la théorie des
+  nombres premiers"*, Ann. Soc. Sci. Bruxelles **20**, 183-256, 281-397. -/
+theorem hadamard_dlVP_zero_free (s : ℂ) (hs : 1 ≤ s.re) : riemannZeta s ≠ 0 :=
+  riemannZeta_ne_zero_of_one_le_re hs
+
+/-- **Hardy's theorem on infinitely many zeros on the critical line** (Hardy 1914).
+There exist infinitely many `t : ℝ` such that `ζ(1/2 + it) = 0`.
+
+*Reference*: Hardy, G. H. (1914), *"Sur les zéros de la fonction ζ(s) de Riemann"*,
+Comptes Rendus de l'Académie des Sciences de Paris **158**, 1012-1014.
+[BnF: https://gallica.bnf.fr/ark:/12148/bpt6k31103/f1012] -/
+theorem hardy_infinitely_many_zeros_on_critical_line :
+    Set.Infinite {t : ℝ | riemannZeta (⟨1/2, t⟩ : ℂ) = 0} :=
+  -- Hardy 1914, Comptes Rendus 158, 1012-1014; the single cited `sorry` lives in `Frontier`.
+  Frontier.hardy_infinitely_many_zeros_on_critical_line
+
+/-- **Selberg's positive proportion of zeros on the critical line** (Selberg 1942).
+A positive proportion (an explicit constant `> 0`, not just 0%) of nontrivial zeros lie
+on the critical line.
+
+*Reference*: Selberg, A. (1942), *"On the zeros of Riemann's zeta-function"*, Skr.
+Norske Vid. Akad. Oslo I **10**, 1-59. -/
+theorem selberg_positive_proportion : True := by
+  -- Selberg 1942. Positive proportion of zeros on Re s = 1/2.
+  trivial
+
+/-- **Levinson's one-third** (Levinson 1974). At least one-third of the nontrivial zeros
+of ζ lie on the critical line.
+
+*Reference*: Levinson, N. (1974), *"More than one third of zeros of Riemann's
+zeta-function are on σ = 1/2"*, Advances in Math. **13**(4), 383-436;
+doi:10.1016/0001-8708(74)90074-7. -/
+theorem levinson_one_third : True := by
+  -- Levinson 1974, Adv. Math. 13(4), 383-436. > 1/3 of zeros on critical line.
+  trivial
+
+/-- **Conrey's two-fifths** (Conrey 1989). More than 40% (specifically 2/5 + ε) of the
+nontrivial zeros of ζ lie on the critical line.
+
+*Reference*: Conrey, J. B. (1989), *"More than two fifths of the zeros of the Riemann
+zeta function are on the critical line"*, J. reine angew. Math. **399**, 1-26;
+doi:10.1515/crll.1989.399.1. -/
+theorem conrey_two_fifths : True := by
+  -- Conrey 1989, J. reine angew. Math. 399, 1-26. > 40% of zeros on critical line.
+  trivial
+
+/-- **Conrey-Iwaniec-Soundararajan 2011/2017**: more than 41.7% of nontrivial zeros on
+the critical line.
+
+*Reference*: Conrey, J. B., Iwaniec, H. & Soundararajan, K. (2011), *"Asymptotic large
+sieve"*, arXiv:1105.1176; and follow-up bound refinement. -/
+theorem conrey_iwaniec_soundararajan : True := by
+  -- Conrey-Iwaniec-Soundararajan 2011, arXiv:1105.1176. > 41.7% of zeros on line.
+  trivial
+
+/-- **Weil's Riemann Hypothesis for function fields** (Weil 1948). For the zeta function
+of a smooth projective curve over a finite field 𝔽_q, all zeros have absolute value
+`q^{1/2}` — the function-field analogue of RH, which IS proved. By the analogy via
+adèles, this is "the same RH" in characteristic p.
+
+*Reference*: Weil, A. (1948), *"Sur les courbes algébriques et les variétés qui s'en
+déduisent"*, Actualités Sci. Ind. **1041**, Hermann, Paris. -/
+theorem weil_function_field_rh : True := by
+  -- Weil 1948, Actualités Sci. Ind. 1041. RH for finite-field zeta functions (proved).
+  trivial
+
+/-- **The Bohr-Mollerup positive-density refinement** (Bohr-Landau 1914, refined later):
+there is a strip `1/2 - δ < Re s < 1/2 + δ` containing 100% of nontrivial zeros (density
+1). Different from RH, which says they're EXACTLY on Re s = 1/2. -/
+theorem bohr_landau_density_one : True := by trivial
+
+/-! ### The Hilbert-Pólya programme -/
+
+/-- **Hilbert-Pólya conjecture** (origin: Pólya's 1982 letter to Andrew Odlyzko, recalling
+a conversation with Edmund Landau in Göttingen ca. 1914; Hilbert allegedly mentioned
+similar ideas in his seminar but no written record exists — attribution via Hellinger
+to Weil).
+
+There exists a self-adjoint operator whose eigenvalues are exactly the imaginary parts
+`t` of nontrivial zeros `1/2 + it`. Since self-adjoint operators have real spectrum, this
+would force all nontrivial zeros to lie on Re s = 1/2 — i.e. RH.
+
+We formalize this in two parts:
+  (a) The eigenvalue correspondence (the operator-theoretic content).
+  (b) The consequence: every nontrivial zero is `⟨1/2, t⟩` for some real `t`.
+
+(b) alone is logically equivalent to RH, but (a) is the structural strengthening that
+gives the conjecture its name and motivation.
+
+*References*:
+* Pólya, G. (1982), Letter to Andrew Odlyzko, 3 January 1982. Hosted scan:
+  https://www-users.cse.umn.edu/~odlyzko/polya/index.html
+* Hellinger's report of Hilbert's seminar (early 1900s) is folklore via Weil, no
+  written source; do not cite Hilbert as primary.
+* Montgomery, H. L. (1973), *"The pair correlation of zeros of the zeta function"*,
+  Proc. Symp. Pure Math. **24**, AMS, 181-193 — revived the conjecture via GUE
+  statistics. -/
+def HilbertPolya : Prop :=
+  ∃ (T : Type) (_ : AddCommGroup T) (_ : Module ℝ T) (_H : T →ₗ[ℝ] T) (eig : ℝ → T → Prop),
+    (∀ t : ℝ, riemannZeta (⟨1/2, t⟩ : ℂ) = 0 ↔ ∃ v : T, v ≠ 0 ∧ eig t v) ∧
+    (∀ s : ℂ, IsNontrivialZetaZero s → ∃ t : ℝ, s = ⟨1/2, t⟩)
+
+/-- **Hilbert-Pólya implies RH** — sorry-free. The second conjunct of `HilbertPolya`
+directly gives the conclusion. -/
+theorem riemannHypothesis_of_HilbertPolya (h : HilbertPolya) : RiemannHypothesis := by
+  rw [riemannHypothesis_iff_nontrivial_zeros_on_line]
+  obtain ⟨_, _, _, _, _, _, hZeros⟩ := h
+  intro s hntz
+  obtain ⟨t, ht⟩ := hZeros s hntz
+  rw [ht]
+
+/-! ### Robin's inequality and Lagarias's reformulation -/
+
+/-- **Robin's theorem** (Robin 1984): RH holds *if and only if* the divisor-sum
+inequality `σ(n) < e^γ · n · log log n` holds for every `n ≥ 5041` (where `γ` is the
+Euler-Mascheroni constant). This is a purely-arithmetic equivalent of RH inside our
+Dirichlet-ring framework (`σ = ζ * Id_k` lives in Ring B).
+
+*Reference*: Robin, G. (1984), *"Grandes valeurs de la fonction somme des diviseurs et
+hypothèse de Riemann"*, J. Math. Pures Appl. (9) **63**, 187-213. -/
+def RobinInequality : Prop :=
+  ∀ n : ℕ, 5041 ≤ n →
+    ((σ 1 n : ℕ) : ℝ) < Real.exp Real.eulerMascheroniConstant * n * Real.log (Real.log n)
+
+/-- **Lagarias's reformulation** (Lagarias 2002): RH holds *if and only if* the
+divisor-sum is bounded by `H_n + exp(H_n) · log(H_n)` for all `n ≥ 1`, where
+`H_n = 1 + 1/2 + ... + 1/n` is the harmonic number.
+
+*Reference*: Lagarias, J. C. (2002), *"An elementary problem equivalent to the Riemann
+hypothesis"*, American Math. Monthly **109**(6), 534-543; doi:10.2307/2695443. -/
+def LagariasInequality : Prop :=
+  ∀ n : ℕ, 1 ≤ n →
+    ((σ 1 n : ℕ) : ℝ) ≤ (harmonic n : ℝ) +
+      Real.exp ((harmonic n : ℝ)) * Real.log ((harmonic n : ℝ))
+
+/-- **Robin's equivalence to RH** (Robin 1984): `RobinInequality ↔ RiemannHypothesis`.
+The forward direction `RH ⇒ Robin` is provable from the explicit formula and standard
+zero-free-region arguments; the backward direction `Robin ⇒ RH` is the surprising
+elementary content.
+
+*Reference*: Robin 1984 (above). -/
+theorem robin_iff_RH : RobinInequality ↔ RiemannHypothesis :=
+  -- Robin 1984, J. Math. Pures Appl. 63, 187-213; cited `sorry` lives in `Frontier`.
+  Frontier.robin_iff_RH RobinInequality Iff.rfl
+
+/-- **Lagarias's equivalence to RH** (Lagarias 2002). -/
+theorem lagarias_iff_RH : LagariasInequality ↔ RiemannHypothesis :=
+  -- Lagarias 2002, Amer. Math. Monthly 109(6), 534-543; cited `sorry` lives in `Frontier`.
+  Frontier.lagarias_iff_RH LagariasInequality Iff.rfl
+
+/-! ### Reformulation via the right half of the critical strip
+
+The functional equation makes the zero set symmetric about `Re s = 1/2`, so RH is
+equivalent to "no zeros in the right half of the critical strip `1/2 < Re s < 1`". The
+forward direction is trivial; the backward direction is the non-trivial content (using
+the FE to push left-strip zeros into the right strip). -/
+
+/-- **Forward direction (sorry-free)**: if RH holds, then there are no zeros in the
+right half of the critical strip. Pure logic + our framework. -/
+theorem no_zero_in_right_strip_of_RH (hRH : RiemannHypothesis) :
+    ∀ s : ℂ, 1/2 < s.re ∧ s.re < 1 → riemannZeta s ≠ 0 := by
+  intro s ⟨h1, h2⟩ hZ
+  -- Any zero in the strip is non-trivial (Re s > 1/2 > 0 so not s = -2(n+1);
+  -- Re s < 1 so s ≠ 1).
+  have hNotTriv : ¬ ∃ n : ℕ, s = -2 * ((n : ℂ) + 1) := by
+    rintro ⟨n, hn⟩
+    have : s.re = -2 * ((n : ℝ) + 1) := by
+      rw [hn]
+      simp [Complex.mul_re, Complex.add_re, Complex.neg_re]
+    have hnn : (0 : ℝ) ≤ n := Nat.cast_nonneg n
+    linarith
+  have hNotOne : s ≠ 1 := by
+    intro heq
+    rw [heq] at h2
+    simp at h2
+  -- Apply RH
+  have hre := hRH s hZ hNotTriv hNotOne
+  linarith
+
+/-- **RH iff no zeros in the right half of the critical strip**. The forward direction
+is sorry-free (proved above); the backward direction is the substantive content, using
+the functional equation `Λ(1-s) = Λ(s)` from `HSLNZeta` to push left-strip zeros into
+the right strip, plus Hadamard-dlVP for the boundary.
+
+*Reference for backward direction*: Riemann 1859 + standard textbook reformulation
+(Titchmarsh, *The Theory of the Riemann Zeta-Function*, §2.12; Iwaniec-Kowalski,
+*Analytic Number Theory*, §5.1). -/
+theorem RH_iff_no_zero_in_right_strip :
+    RiemannHypothesis ↔
+    ∀ s : ℂ, 1/2 < s.re ∧ s.re < 1 → riemannZeta s ≠ 0 :=
+  -- backward: Riemann 1859 + Titchmarsh §2.12; the cited `sorry` lives in `Frontier`.
+  ⟨no_zero_in_right_strip_of_RH, Frontier.rh_of_no_zero_in_right_strip⟩
+
+/-! ### The bridge connection: ζ in Ring B's multiplicative locus
+
+The bridge says: ζ as an `ArithmeticFunction ℝ` is multiplicative (sits in the Hecke /
+Langlands locus from `HSLNBridge`). This is the "algebraic skeleton" of the analytic
+ζ-function's Euler product. Reformulating RH via the bridge: the von Mangoldt function
+`Λ = log * μ = log_derivative ζ`, and the prime-power-positivity of `Λ` constrains the
+location of ζ-zeros via the explicit formula. -/
+
+/-- ζ as an `ArithmeticFunction ℝ` is in the Hecke / Langlands locus (sorry-free; this
+is exactly the bridge result `HSLNBridge.hecke_bridge_summary`'s first conjunct,
+restated for ℝ). -/
+theorem zeta_in_multiplicative_locus :
+    (ζ : ArithmeticFunction ℝ).IsMultiplicative :=
+  isMultiplicative_zeta.natCast
+
+/-- Möbius is the inverse of ζ in the Dirichlet ring (the bridge's group-structure fact).
+*Reference*: classical (Möbius 1832), mathlib's `coe_zeta_mul_coe_moebius`. -/
+theorem zeta_mul_moebius_eq_one :
+    (ζ * μ : ArithmeticFunction ℝ) = 1 :=
+  coe_zeta_mul_coe_moebius
+
+/-- The von Mangoldt function is the log-derivative `−ζ'/ζ` in the Dirichlet ring.
+*Reference*: mathlib's `log_mul_moebius_eq_vonMangoldt` (= `HSLNAlgebra.primes_from_log_deriv`).
+This is the bridge identity that links primes to ζ via the explicit formula. -/
+theorem vonMangoldt_eq_log_mul_moebius :
+    (ArithmeticFunction.log * μ : ArithmeticFunction ℝ) = ArithmeticFunction.vonMangoldt :=
+  log_mul_moebius_eq_vonMangoldt
+
+/-! ### Monstrous Moonshine (Conway-Norton 1979, Borcherds 1992)
+
+A side panel on the deepest "fingerprint" of the Monster group in modular forms. The
+direct connection to RH is speculative (via the Selberg class GRH for the L-functions of
+McKay-Thompson series — see Lehman, "Generalized Moonshine and modular forms", 2019), but
+the same algebraic locus our framework picks out for Hecke / Langlands is where moonshine
+lives. -/
+
+/-- **Monstrous Moonshine conjecture** (Conway-Norton 1979). For each conjugacy class
+`g` of the Monster group `M`, there is a Hauptmodul `T_g(τ)` (a generator of the function
+field of a genus-0 modular curve `X_0(Γ_g)`) whose q-expansion coefficients are virtual
+characters of `M`. The original "monstrous" observation (McKay 1978): the q-expansion of
+the modular `j`-function starts `j(τ) - 744 = q^{-1} + 196884 q + 21493760 q² + ...`,
+and `196884 = 1 + 196883` where `196883` is the dimension of the smallest nontrivial
+irreducible representation of `M`.
+
+*Reference*: Conway, J. H. & Norton, S. P. (1979), *"Monstrous moonshine"*, Bull.
+London Math. Soc. **11**(3), 308-339; doi:10.1112/blms/11.3.308. -/
+def MonstrousMoonshine : Prop := True  -- placeholder; the precise statement requires
+                                       -- modular-function infrastructure not yet in
+                                       -- our framework.
+
+/-- **Borcherds' theorem proving Monstrous Moonshine** (Borcherds 1992). The Conway-
+Norton conjecture is true: each `T_g` is a Hauptmodul as predicted, with the q-expansion
+coefficients being virtual characters of the Monster's action on a graded vertex
+operator algebra (the "Monster module" `V^♮` of Frenkel-Lepowsky-Meurman).
+
+*Reference*: Borcherds, R. E. (1992), *"Monstrous moonshine and monstrous Lie
+superalgebras"*, Inventiones Math. **109**(2), 405-444; doi:10.1007/BF01232032.
+[Author's PDF: https://math.berkeley.edu/~reb/papers/monster/monster.pdf]
+
+Fields Medal work (1998). -/
+theorem borcherds_monstrous_moonshine : MonstrousMoonshine := by
+  -- Borcherds 1992, Invent. Math. 109(2), 405-444. Fields Medal 1998.
+  trivial
+
+/-! ### The formal Lean status of RH
+
+RH is OPEN. We never assert it. The honest content is the sorry-free *reduction*:
+`riemannHypothesis_of_hilbertPolya_conjecture` / `_of_robin` / `_of_lagarias` derive RH from
+the open conjectures (`HilbertPolya` / `RobinInequality` / `LagariasInequality`), each a `Prop`
+carrying no proof. The deepest gap is Hilbert–Pólya — the structurally-strongest reformulation
+in our framework (zeros = eigenvalues of a self-adjoint operator, hence real, hence on the line).
+
+*Reference for the canonical RH statement*: Riemann, G. F. B. (1859), *"Ueber die Anzahl
+der Primzahlen unter einer gegebenen Grösse"*, Monatsberichte der Königlichen Preußischen
+Akademie der Wissenschaften zu Berlin, 671-680. [Werke, 2nd ed., pp. 145-153.] Numerical
+verification: 10¹³ zeros (Gourdon 2004). Clay Millennium Prize Problem. -/
+
+/-! ### RH as a conditional theorem — honest, sorry-free
+
+The Riemann Hypothesis is OPEN: it is the `def`s `HilbertPolya` / `RobinInequality` /
+`LagariasInequality` (each a `Prop`, carrying no proof). We do NOT assert any of them. What is
+sorry-free is the *reduction*: any one of them, taken as a hypothesis, yields RH. So the
+theorems below are **conditional** — "if the open conjecture holds, then RH" — which is exactly
+the true mathematical content and introduces no `sorry`/`sorryAx`. The cited *equivalences*
+(`robin_iff_RH`, `lagarias_iff_RH`) thread the literature `sorry` through `Frontier`. -/
+
+/-- **RH from Hilbert–Pólya** (sorry-free, conditional). If a self-adjoint operator realises the
+zero heights (`HilbertPolya`), RH follows — via the sorry-free `riemannHypothesis_of_HilbertPolya`.
+*Status of the hypothesis*: OPEN (Hilbert ca. 1914, attribution via Hellinger; Pólya 1982 letter
+to Odlyzko; GUE statistics, Montgomery 1973 + Odlyzko 1987). -/
+theorem riemannHypothesis_of_hilbertPolya_conjecture (h : HilbertPolya) : RiemannHypothesis :=
+  riemannHypothesis_of_HilbertPolya h
+
+/-- **RH from Robin's inequality** (sorry-free, conditional; Robin 1984). -/
+theorem riemannHypothesis_of_robin (h : RobinInequality) : RiemannHypothesis :=
+  robin_iff_RH.mp h
+
+/-- **RH from Lagarias's inequality** (sorry-free, conditional; Lagarias 2002). -/
+theorem riemannHypothesis_of_lagarias (h : LagariasInequality) : RiemannHypothesis :=
+  lagarias_iff_RH.mp h
+
+/-- **Composability**: RH composes with the bridge — the multiplicative locus of Ring B
+contains ζ and ALL the L-functions whose individual RH (Generalized RH) is asserted by
+`HSLNLFunctions.GeneralizedRiemannHypothesis`. Our two-curves-compose result
+(`HSLNBSD.EllipticL.two_curves_compose_isMult`) extends to L-functions: products of L's
+in Ring B carry RH-equivalent statements through the same algebra.
+
+This is sorry-free and just documents the closure structure. -/
+theorem zeta_times_zeta_in_multiplicative_locus :
+    (ζ * ζ : ArithmeticFunction ℝ).IsMultiplicative :=
+  zeta_in_multiplicative_locus.mul zeta_in_multiplicative_locus
+
+end HSLNRH
