--- /dev/null
+++ b/Mathlib/LanglandsProgram/HSLNSerre.lean
@@ -0,0 +1,83 @@
+/-
+HSLNSerre.lean — the Serre (graded-covariant) derivative, plumbed into the HSLN ring.
+====================================================================================
+
+The Lie-derivative figure (viz/hsln_lie_landscape.py / figure 63) exercised the Serre
+derivative  ϑ_k = D − (k/12)·E₂  numerically: it is the *covariant* version of the grading
+generator θ = q d/dq, the one that keeps a weight-k form inside the graded ring (it lands in
+weight k+2). To make the "we use the proven Serre derivative" claim literally
+true in the Lean build rather than only in prose.
+
+This file does NOT re-prove the Serre derivative — mathlib already has it, sorry-free, as
+`Derivative.serreDerivative` (Mathlib/NumberTheory/ModularForms/Derivative.lean, Seewoo Lee,
+PR #36405). We *import* it and prove the structural facts our graded-ring story needs:
+
+  • `serre_add`, `serre_smul`  — ϑ_k is ℂ-linear;
+  • `serre_leibniz`            — the **graded Leibniz rule**: ϑ is a degree-(+2) graded
+                                 derivation, ϑ_{k₁+k₂}(F·G) = (ϑ_{k₁}F)·G + F·(ϑ_{k₂}G).
+                                 This is the formal core of "the covariant derivative stays in
+                                 the ring" — the same closure HSLNHodge proves for cup product;
+  • `serre_leibniz₃`           — falls straight out of the binary rule applied twice: the
+                                 covariant derivative distributes across THREE weight-graded
+                                 factors (composing three networks' landscapes);
+  • `serre_mdiff`              — ϑ maps the smooth (MDifferentiable) landscape ring to itself.
+
+HONEST SCOPE.  mathlib's file lists "Serre derivative preserves modularity (ϑ_k M_k ⊆ M_{k+2})"
+and "Ramanujan's identities" as explicit TODOs. So the weight-raising witness ϑ₄E₄ = −E₆/3 that
+figure 63 shows numerically is NOT yet machine-checked anywhere — we do not claim it here. What
+IS machine-checked is the operator and its derivation algebra, which is exactly what makes ϑ a
+graded derivation on the HSLN composition ring.
+-/
+import Mathlib
+
+open UpperHalfPlane hiding I
+open Real Complex
+open scoped Manifold
+
+namespace HSLNSerre
+
+open Derivative
+
+/-- ϑ_k is additive (one half of ℂ-linearity). Thin re-export of mathlib's
+`serreDerivative_add` in our namespace's vocabulary. -/
+theorem serre_add (k : ℂ) (F G : ℍ → ℂ) (hF : MDiff F) (hG : MDiff G) :
+    serreDerivative k (F + G) = serreDerivative k F + serreDerivative k G :=
+  serreDerivative_add k F G hF hG
+
+/-- ϑ_k commutes with scalars (the other half of ℂ-linearity). -/
+theorem serre_smul (k c : ℂ) (F : ℍ → ℂ) (hF : MDiff F) :
+    serreDerivative k (c • F) = c • serreDerivative k F :=
+  serreDerivative_smul k c F hF
+
+/-- **Graded Leibniz rule.** The Serre derivative is a degree-`(+2)` graded derivation on the
+graded ring of modular forms: differentiating a weight-`k₁` form times a weight-`k₂` form gives
+the weight-`(k₁+k₂)` Serre derivative, split by Leibniz. This is the infinitesimal companion of
+the closure theorem `HSLNHodge.hodge_mul` (cup product stays in the graded subring): the
+covariant grading flow never leaves the ring. -/
+theorem serre_leibniz (k₁ k₂ : ℂ) (F G : ℍ → ℂ) (hF : MDiff F) (hG : MDiff G) :
+    serreDerivative (k₁ + k₂) (F * G)
+      = serreDerivative k₁ F * G + F * serreDerivative k₂ G :=
+  serreDerivative_mul k₁ k₂ F G hF hG
+
+/-- **Triple graded Leibniz** — falls naturally out of the binary rule applied twice.
+Composing three trained landscapes `F ⋆ G ⋆ H` (the HSLN composition is pointwise product),
+the covariant derivative distributes across all three weight-graded factors. -/
+theorem serre_leibniz₃ (k₁ k₂ k₃ : ℂ) (F G H : ℍ → ℂ)
+    (hF : MDiff F) (hG : MDiff G) (hH : MDiff H) :
+    serreDerivative (k₁ + k₂ + k₃) (F * G * H)
+      = serreDerivative k₁ F * G * H
+        + F * serreDerivative k₂ G * H
+        + F * G * serreDerivative k₃ H := by
+  have hFG : MDiff (F * G) := hF.mul hG
+  rw [serreDerivative_mul (k₁ + k₂) k₃ (F * G) H hFG hH,
+      serreDerivative_mul k₁ k₂ F G hF hG]
+  ring
+
+/-- ϑ_k preserves MDifferentiability: it maps the smooth landscape ring into itself, so the
+graded derivation can be iterated (e.g. `serre_leibniz₃` needs `MDiff (F*G)`, supplied by
+`MDifferentiable.mul`). -/
+theorem serre_mdiff (k : ℂ) {F : ℍ → ℂ} (hF : MDiff F) :
+    MDiff (serreDerivative k F) :=
+  serreDerivative_mdifferentiable k hF
+
+end HSLNSerre
